## Root cause

`padStart(input, n)` in **/luxon/src/impl/util.js:101** padded the *whole string representation* of the number, so the leading `-` was counted as one of the `n` digits:

```js
if (input.toString().length < n) {
  return ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);   // padStart(-1, 4) -> "0000-1".slice(-4) === "00-1"
}
```

`Formatter.num()` (src/impl/formatter.js:119) calls `padStart` on the `forceSimple` path used by `toISODate`/`toISO`/`toISOWeekDate`/`toRFC2822`/`toHTTP`, and `PolyNumberFormatter.format()` (src/impl/locale.js:169) uses it for `toFormat` whenever Intl fast-numbers apply (en/de/fr… and the no-Intl degrade path). Positive years were unaffected because no sign is present; negative years got the sign shifted into the padding.

## Fix (only file changed: /luxon/src/impl/util.js, lines 101-110)

Pad the magnitude and prepend the sign separately — this also matches `Intl.NumberFormat`'s `minimumIntegerDigits` semantics (verified: `Intl.NumberFormat("en-US",{useMinimumIntegerDigits:4}).format(-1) === "-0001"`), so the Intl and polyfill paths now agree:

```js
export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
  const isNeg = input < 0;
  let padded;
  if (isNeg) {
    padded = "-" + ("" + -input).padStart(n, "0");
  } else {
    padded = ("" + input).padStart(n, "0");
  }
  return padded;
}
```

No other src file needed changing (`formatOffset` already passes `Math.abs(hours)`; `toISODate`'s `"+"` prefix for years > 9999 is untouched). No test file was modified (`git status test/` is empty).

